      "worked": "Assume both branches must be defined throughout their stated intervals, so $a\\ne0$.\n\nEach branch is continuous within its interval. At the join $x=a$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(a)&=a^2+2\\\\f_L(a)&=4-\\frac{a^2}{a}\\\\f_L(a)&=4-a\\\\f_R(a)&=a^2+2\\end{align*}$$\n\nContinuity requires $f(a)=f_L(a)=f_R(a)$, so\n\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2+2&=4-a\\\\a^2+a-2&=0\\\\(a+2)(a-1)&=0\\\\a&=1\\text{ or }a=-2\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $a=1$, $f(1)=f_L(1)=f_R(1)=3$.\n\nFor $a=-2$, $f(-2)=f_L(-2)=f_R(-2)=6$.\n\nBoth values are nonzero and make the branches meet. Hence $a=1$ or $a=-2$ gives continuity at every point under the stated assumption.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
